notes.body:
My account was locked for some reason but I now have access again.

@johnthacker I added an example packet capture ([diag-add-sync_20260201190024.pcapng](/uploads/8f58e5da5b3fe6755e6b05ef084e478b/diag-add-sync_20260201190024.pcapng)) and noted in my reply to your request that packet 67 has valid, invalid, and missing time fields to illustrate how they are handled.

Pipeline 2315453350 is currently running.

Looking at the capture file you posted, it is strange to me that you appear not to be clearing the Info column - the only time to write to it (line packet_p4rpc.c:836) is to append the information still showing from the TCP layer..

![image](/uploads/80c48f8765c38b172fd012f441b5190a/image.png){width=890 height=311}

Would you not find it useful to clear it, then write at least 'func' of each P4RPC that appears within the frame?

The way you build argbuf at around line 557 is over-complicated. I confess that I had never used wmem_strbuf myself before, but it simplifies the code quite a bit. Not sure how I can attach the patch I used, but the key part is this:

```
    // make our "var=val" string
    wmem_strbuf_t *arg_strbuf = wmem_strbuf_new(pinfo->pool, "");
    wmem_strbuf_append_printf(arg_strbuf, "%s = {%s}",
                              (*varname ? varname : (const uint8_t *)"<none>"),
                              varval );
```

Then to access the string, its just varval-\>str.

Note that wmem_strbuf_new defaults to size DEFAULT_MINIMUM_SIZE, which is currently 16 bytes. You could use (say)

wmem_strbuf_len(pinfo-\>pool, "", 32);

which would then hardly ever need to be re-allocated/grown for your usecase.

